## From bytes to agents

You started with a guessing game: what word comes next? Everything since has been about how a
machine plays that game, and what gets built around it. Zoom in and out of the whole picture; each
part links back to the chapter that explains it.

<WholePicture />

<At levels="simple">

In short: a language model gives a **probability** for every possible next token (<ChapterRef to="guessing-game" through="probability-and-sampling" />).
Text is split into **tokens**, each token becomes a list of numbers, and a stack of blocks refines
those numbers, with **attention** letting each token use the ones before it (<ChapterRef to="text-is-numbers" through="the-transformer-block" />). **Training**
sets the numbers inside by making the right next tokens more likely (<ChapterRef to="a-neuron" through="learning-from-mistakes" />), and writing is a **loop** of
predicting and picking (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />). Tuning makes the model an **assistant** (<ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />). A prompt is the
start of a document for the model to continue (<ChapterRef to="prompts-as-documents" through="where-prompts-break" />). A **harness** around the model adds
memory and picks which model answers (<ChapterRef to="the-model-is-not-the-product" through="routing" />), and lets it use **tools** safely (<ChapterRef to="tools" through="trust-and-guardrails" />). Other kinds of models change one piece and
keep the rest (<ChapterRef to="encoders" through="decision-models" />).

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

In one paragraph: a language model gives a **probability** for every possible next token (<ChapterRef to="guessing-game" through="probability-and-sampling" />).
Text becomes **tokens** (<ChapterRef to="text-is-numbers" />, <ChapterRef to="tokens" bare />), each token becomes a **vector** (<ChapterRef to="embeddings" />), and a stack of
**transformer blocks** refines those vectors, with **attention** letting each token use earlier ones
(<ChapterRef to="the-context-problem" through="the-transformer-block" />). The numbers inside are learned by **training** that nudges millions or billions of
weights to make the right tokens more likely (<ChapterRef to="a-neuron" through="learning-from-mistakes" />, <ChapterRef to="scale" bare />). Generating text is a **loop** of
predicting and picking (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />). Those weights can be rounded to fewer bits to save memory (<ChapterRef to="quantization" />),
and a small model can draft tokens for a big one to check, so it writes the same text faster
(<ChapterRef to="speculative-decoding" />). Tuning turns the model into an **assistant** (<ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />), and reasoning
models are tuned to write their working first (<ChapterRef to="reasoning-models" />). Prompting is choosing the document the model
continues: the wording, examples, instructions, and layout all shift what comes next, often in ways
you have to test to find out (<ChapterRef to="prompts-as-documents" through="where-prompts-break" />). A **harness** wraps the model with prompts, context management, caching,
and a choice of which model answers (<ChapterRef to="the-model-is-not-the-product" through="routing" />). It can also give the model **tools** and run it in an agent
loop, with guardrails against prompt injection (<ChapterRef to="tools" through="trust-and-guardrails" />). Other kinds
of models change one piece and keep the rest: encoders read both ways, mixtures of experts use part
of themselves per token, recurrent models keep a fixed memory, vision models read patches, and
diffusion models paint from noise, and decision models predict moves instead of words (<ChapterRef to="encoders" through="decision-models" />).

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The whole model in a few lines

The In depth passages add up to all of GPT-2 small. For tokens $x_1, \dots, x_n$, each starts as
its row of the token table plus its position's row (<ChapterRef to="embeddings" />, <ChapterRef to="the-transformer-block" bare />):

$$h_i = W_E[x_i] + W_P[i]$$

Each of the 12 blocks then updates every vector with attention and an MLP (<ChapterRef to="attention" />, <ChapterRef to="the-transformer-block" bare />):

$$h' = h + \operatorname{Attn}(\operatorname{LN}(h)), \qquad h'' = h' + \operatorname{MLP}(\operatorname{LN}(h'))$$

The last token's final vector is scored against every token in the table and turned into
probabilities, with temperature $T$ (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />):

$$p(\,\cdot \mid x_1, \dots, x_n) = \operatorname{softmax}\big(\operatorname{LN}_f(h_n)\, W_E^{\top} / T\big)$$

Training lowers the cross-entropy by nudging every weight downhill (<ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />):

$$L = -\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \ln p(x_i \mid x_1, \dots, x_{i-1}), \qquad w \leftarrow w - \eta \, \frac{\partial L}{\partial w}$$

Tuning changes the weights, not these lines. Prompts and harnesses change the tokens that go in,
and what happens to the ones that come out. The other kinds of models each change one line: the
attention mask, the MLP, attention itself, what a token stands for (a patch of a picture, or a step
of a game), or how the output is made.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A language model predicts the next token, nothing more, and does it very well at scale.
- What looks like a chat, a memory, or an agent is a harness calling that model in loops.
- Most quirks, from made-up facts to miscounted letters, follow from those two facts.
- Other models swap one piece, like the attention mask, the MLP, or what counts as a token, and
  keep the rest of the recipe.
- A prompt is the start of a document: everything in it shifts the next token, so test it.

<At levels="deep">

- GPT-2 fits in a few lines: embed, 12 blocks of attention and MLP, a softmax over the token table,
  and cross-entropy for training.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** those are the pieces. In what order do they run when you send a message?
